"The benchmark columns refer to different documented stages: 14,024 is the paper's selected immigrant sample, whereas the observed migration row covers all imported records; 8,638 is the paper's participating-origin restriction, whereas the observed 2018 row requires an available origin-sex FAI standard. These are diagnostic comparisons, not claims of equivalent filters. Country codes pool Belgian regions and retain UK constituent nations. The complete 2018 count is only a candidate based on observed raw covariates and standards, not a verified analysis N or power input.","",
"**Construction used for this diagnostic:** BMI from kg/cm; sex-specific WHO 2007 LMS parameters linearly interpolated at age*12 months; WHO tail adjustment beyond +/-3; paper's five actual-status categories; FAI=perceived minus actual. A provisional z screen [-5,5] is applied equally across waves, with unscreened counts retained. This screen and LMS convention have not been verified against author code. FAS checks here validate six item codes only; quintile construction and age scaling are not yet implemented. Country means are unweighted, include immigrants, and use valid FAI reference records before immigrant restrictions. Both person-weighted and country-weighted sex-specific grand centring are included in the country table; neither has been selected as the verified paper convention. The paper's median/SD wording for its BMI covariate remains unresolved.","",
"WHO source: [official BMI-for-age reference tables](https://www.who.int/tools/growth-reference-data-for-5to19-years/indicators/bmi-for-age) and [LMS/tail computation instructions](https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/child-growth/growth-reference-5-19-years/computation.pdf). The compact data/who2007_bmi_lms.csv retains Month/L/M/S from the official boys/girls expanded XLSX tables downloaded on 26 October 2026; no participant data are sent externally. Computation is checked against WHO's three worked examples.","",
"**2018 status:** descriptive extension only. Because 2014 immigrant-model reproduction failed the data-availability gate, no 2018 regression coefficients are estimated. Reproduction requires detailed 2014 origin data/national mappings and clarification of original exclusions/WHO implementation before claiming Tables 1-4 are reproducible. Model/power complexity review remains pending. Outcomes have now been examined descriptively; no prospective preregistration is claimed.","",
"[Sample reconciliation table](sample_reconciliation.csv) | [Country-sex FAI table](country_fai.csv)","",
"![Reference sample reconciliation](reference_sample.png)","",
"![Country-sex FAI means in 2014 and 2018](country_fai.png)")
}
if (file.exists(file.path(root,"results","country_fai.csv"))) {
cs <- read.csv(file.path(root,"results","country_fai.csv"))
a <- cs[cs$wave==2014,]
boys <- a[a$sex==1,]; girls <- a[a$sex==2,]
paired <- merge(boys,girls,by="country",suffixes=c("_boys","_girls"))
lowest <- boys[which.min(boys$mean_fai),]; highest <- girls[which.max(girls$mean_fai),]
gap_count <- sum(paired$mean_fai_girls>paired$mean_fai_boys)
r2 <- cor(paired$mean_fai_boys,paired$mean_fai_girls)^2
n14 <- sum(a$n)
lines <- c(lines,"","## Direct comparison with paper_to_be_replicated.pdf","",
"Checked against Section 2.1 (p.3), Sections 2.2-2.3 and Tables 1-3 (pp.4-5), Figures 1-2 (p.6), and Table 4 (p.7). Results below concern the current public release and the documented provisional scoring, not author-equivalent analytic data.","",
"### Reference population and Figures 1-2","",
"| Paper claim / statistic | Paper | Current 2014 check | Assessment |","|---|---|---|---|",
sprintf("| Reference-population N | 166,798 | %s; difference %+d (%.2f%%) | Does not match under current construction |",format(n14,big.mark=","),n14-166798,100*(n14-166798)/166798),
sprintf("| Participating geographic units | 41 | %d after pooling Belgian regions | Lithuania in paper Table 2 is absent from supplied release |",length(unique(a$country))),
sprintf("| Lowest boys' country mean (Section 3.1) | Greece, approximately -0.4 | %s, %.3f | Country and rounded magnitude can be compared descriptively |",lowest$country,lowest$mean_fai),
sprintf("| Highest girls' country mean (Section 3.1) | Poland, approximately +0.6 | %s, %.3f | Country and rounded magnitude can be compared descriptively |",highest$country,highest$mean_fai),
sprintf("| Girls' mean exceeds boys' within country | All 41 countries; paper reports significance | %d of %d paired countries have a positive mean difference | Direction only; significance has not been reproduced |",gap_count,nrow(paired)),
sprintf("| Shared variance between country means by sex | Approximately 68%% | %.1f%% (unweighted squared Pearson correlation across %d paired countries) | Descriptive comparison with different coverage; not the paper's exact result |",100*r2,nrow(paired)),
"| Girls significantly above zero | 37 of 41 countries | Not estimated | Point-estimate signs do not reproduce confidence-interval tests |",
"| Boys significantly above / below zero | 9 / 24 countries | Not estimated | No survey-adjusted intervals or matching original inference available |","",
"The current figure overlays 2014 and 2018 point means. The paper's Figures 1-2 show 2014 means with 95% intervals. Thus our figure is a diagnostic comparison, not a numerical reproduction of those figures; no exact country values were inferred by digitising the PDF.","",
"### Tables 1-3: immigrant sample","",
"| Published target | Paper value | Current verification |","|---|---|---|",
"| Complete-case immigrant sample | 8,124 | Not reconstructed: exact 2014 origins unavailable |",
"| Generations 1.5 / second | 2,603 / 5,521 | Not verified on the published eligible sample |",
"| Receiving / origin countries | 23 / 41 | Country-specific immigrant tables not reproduced |",
"| FAI mean (SD), Table 3 | 0.19 (0.89) | Not compared with full-reference means: different population |",
"| Age mean (SD), Table 3 | 13.83 (1.65) | Not verified on the published immigrant sample |",
"| FAS III quintile mean (SD), Table 3 | 3.09 (1.43) | Published quintile algorithm and eligible sample unresolved |",
"| BMI z-score mean (SD), Table 3 | -0.01 (1.22) | Eligible sample and author scoring unresolved |",
"| Girls, Table 3 | 51% | Not verified on the published immigrant sample |","",
"**Important construction discrepancy:** Table 3 reports a minimum BMI z-score of -5.56. Our provisional screen excludes WHO-LMS z below -5, so it must not be described as the paper's exclusion rule. The paper's BMI covariate is described as median/SD based, whereas our diagnostic uses WHO LMS; these may not be the same scale. The unscreened reference count is also retained in the reconciliation table. Neither the missing Lithuania records nor the screen alone can be asserted to explain the entire N discrepancy.","",
"### Table 4: regression targets (not re-estimated)","",
"| Term | Model 1 coefficient [95% CI] | Model 2 coefficient [95% posterior interval] |","|---|---|---|",
"| Standardised age | -0.03 [-0.05, 0.00] | -0.03 [-0.05, 0.00] |",
"| Girl | 0.34 [0.31, 0.38] | 0.34 [0.30, 0.37] |",
"| Girl x age | 0.11 [0.07, 0.14] | 0.11 [0.07, 0.14] |",
"| FAS | 0.01 [0.00, 0.02] | 0.01 [0.00, 0.02] |",
"| Generation 1.5 | -0.01 [-0.05, 0.03] | -0.02 [-0.04, 0.08] |",
"| One native parent | 0.06 [0.02, 0.10] | 0.07 [0.03, 0.11] |",
"| BMI z-score | -0.29 [-0.31, -0.28] | -0.29 [-0.31, -0.28] |",
"| Receiving standard | 0.71 [0.54, 0.89] | 0.80 [0.52, 1.07] |",
"| Origin standard | 0.17 [0.03, 0.32] | 0.04 [-0.19, 0.26] |",
"| Generation 1.5 x receiving | Not included | -0.19 [-0.52, 0.16] |",
"| Generation 1.5 x origin | Not included | 0.24 [-0.11, 0.60] |",
"| Receiving-country variance | 0.002 | 0.001 |",
"| Origin-country variance | 0 | 0 |",
"| Individual variance | 0.595 | 0.595 |",
"| Observations | 8,124 | 8,124 |","",
"These entries are transcribed published targets, not fitted results. Model 1 uses Stata IGLS; Model 2 uses MLwiN MCMC. The R lme4 helper does not reproduce those estimation procedures. Rounded endpoints and significance symbols in the paper should not be reverse-engineered into exact p-values. The paper interprets the receiving association as stronger from its intervals, but a receiving-minus-origin contrast in our analysis requires joint coefficient covariance, which Table 4 does not provide.","",
"**Conclusion for power:** some reference-population patterns can be checked descriptively, but neither the eligible 2014 immigrant sample nor its regression results are reproduced. The candidate 2018 complete-input count of 13,858 is not a substitute for validated usable N. Focal partial r and design-appropriate Tier 1/Tier 2 power remain pending the documented inputs and scientific-lead review.")
}
writeLines(lines, file.path(root, "results", "power_assessment.md"))
print(earlier, row.names = FALSE)
print(effects, row.names = FALSE)
message("Existing assessment updated; see results/ for available tables and figures. Complex-design power remains pending.")
}
if ("--power-only" %in% args) {
run_power()
quit(save = "no", status = 0)
}
for (folder in c("results", "environment"))
dir.create(file.path(root, folder), showWarnings = FALSE)
reproduction_results <- run_reproduction()
run_power()
# Downstream helpers: not called by the audit. Use only after the protocol is
# resolved and approved derived data have been independently validated.
construct_fai <- function(perceived, who_bmi_z) {
stopifnot(length(perceived) == length(who_bmi_z))
perceived[!perceived %in% 1:5] <- NA_real_
actual <- cut(who_bmi_z, c(-Inf, -2, -1, 1, 2, Inf), right = FALSE, labels = FALSE)
perceived - actual
}
fit_crossclassified <- function(approved_data) {
if (!requireNamespace("lme4", quietly = TRUE)) stop("Install lme4 in the approved analysis environment.")
required <- c("fai", "receiving_standard", "origin_standard", "age_z", "girl", "fas_quintile", "bmi_z", "generation15", "one_native_parent", "receiving", "origin")
stopifnot(all(required %in% names(approved_data)), all(complete.cases(approved_data[required])))
m1 <- lme4::lmer(fai ~ receiving_standard + origin_standard + age_z * girl +
fas_quintile + bmi_z + generation15 + one_native_parent +
(1 | receiving) + (1 | origin), data = approved_data, REML = FALSE)
m2 <- update(m1, . ~ . + generation15:receiving_standard + generation15:origin_standard)
b <- lme4::fixef(m1); V <- as.matrix(vcov(m1))
L <- setNames(rep(0, length(b)), names(b)); L["receiving_standard"] <- 1; L["origin_standard"] <- -1
estimate <- sum(L * b); se <- sqrt(drop(t(L) %*% V %*% L))
list(model1 = m1, model2 = m2, contrast = data.frame(estimate = estimate, se = se,
lower_normal_approx = estimate - qnorm(.975) * se, upper_normal_approx = estimate + qnorm(.975) * se),
singular = c(lme4::isSingular(m1), lme4::isSingular(m2)))
# lmer ML is an approximation to the published IGLS/MCMC analyses.
# Normal intervals are descriptive, not an approved country-level inference rule.
}
# Replication base: paper_to_be_replicated.pdf, Kern et al. (2020),
# Sections 1.4 and 2.1-2.3, Tables 1-4, Figures 1-2.
# Secondary data power.pdf governs preliminary effect-size/power assessment.
# Run from project root: Rscript code/replication.R
# Base R execution uses the cached official WHO reference; raw files are unchanged.
args <- commandArgs(trailingOnly = TRUE)
positional <- args[!grepl("^--", args)]
find_project_root <- function(explicit = NULL) {
is_project <- function(path) file.exists(file.path(path,"Replication_protocol.md")) &&
file.exists(file.path(path,"paper_to_be_replicated.pdf")) && dir.exists(file.path(path,"data"))
if (length(explicit)) {
path <- normalizePath(explicit[1], mustWork=TRUE)
if (!is_project(path)) stop("The supplied project path must contain Replication_protocol.md, paper_to_be_replicated.pdf and data/: ",path,call.=FALSE)
return(path)
}
script_arg <- grep("^--file=",commandArgs(trailingOnly=FALSE),value=TRUE)
script_paths <- sub("^--file=","",script_arg)
# source() records its input file on the calling stack (including RStudio Source).
source_paths <- unlist(lapply(sys.frames(),function(frame) {
if (exists("ofile",envir=frame,inherits=FALSE)) get("ofile",envir=frame,inherits=FALSE) else NULL
}),use.names=FALSE)
paths <- c(source_paths,script_paths)
# Run-selection in RStudio may have neither --file nor source() context.
if (requireNamespace("rstudioapi",quietly=TRUE) && rstudioapi::isAvailable())
paths <- c(paths,tryCatch(rstudioapi::getSourceEditorContext()$path,error=function(e) character()))
paths <- paths[!is.na(paths) & nzchar(paths)]
starts <- c(dirname(paths),getwd())
for (start in starts) {
path <- normalizePath(start,mustWork=FALSE)
if (!dir.exists(path)) next
repeat {
if (is_project(path)) return(path)
parent <- dirname(path)
if (identical(parent,path)) break
path <- parent
}
}
stop("Cannot locate the replication project. Run/source code/replication.R, or pass the project directory as the first Rscript argument. Searched from script locations and: ",getwd(),call.=FALSE)
}
root <- find_project_root(positional)
# Published targets, not observed results or imposed sample restrictions.
paper_spec <- list(
original_wave = 2014L, temporal_replication_wave = 2018L,
reference_N = 166798L, reference_countries = 41L,
migration_N = 14024L, participating_origin_N = 8638L,
complete_case_exclusions = 514L, model_N = 8124L,
generation15_N = 2603L, generation2_N = 5521L,
receiving_countries = c("Albania", "Austria", "Belgium", "Bulgaria",
"Croatia", "Czech Republic", "Denmark", "Estonia", "Finland", "Germany",
"Greece", "Iceland", "Ireland", "Italy", "Luxembourg", "Portugal",
"Republic of Moldova", "Romania", "Scotland", "Slovenia", "Spain", "Ukraine", "Wales"),
outcome = "Perceived status minus WHO-reference actual status; range -4 to 4",
origin_rule = "Own birthplace for 1.5 generation; maternal birthplace for second generation, falling back to paternal if maternal is missing or receiving country",
reference_rule = "All adolescents with valid inputs across participating countries, not just immigrants; country-sex mean FAI centred on overall sex-specific mean",
original_model1_estimator = "Stata IGLS",
original_model2_estimator = "MLwiN MCMC"
)
stopifnot(length(paper_spec$receiving_countries) == 23L,
paper_spec$participating_origin_N - paper_spec$complete_case_exclusions == paper_spec$model_N,
paper_spec$generation15_N + paper_spec$generation2_N == paper_spec$model_N)
# Power-only mode updates only the existing assessment. No audit files are written.
classify_iso_2018 <- function(d, uk = c("detailed", "GB")) {
uk <- match.arg(uk)
clean <- function(x) {
x <- toupper(trimws(x))
x[x %in% c("", ".", "XX", "ZZ", "UNKNOWN", "99")] <- NA_character_
x[!is.na(x) & !grepl("^[A-Z]{2}(-[A-Z0-9]+)?$", x)] <- NA_character_
# Belgium's survey regions map to the paper's country unit.
x[x %in% c("BE-VLG", "BE-WAL")] <- "BE"
if (uk == "GB") x[!is.na(x) & grepl("^GB-", x)] <- "GB"
x
}
receiving <- clean(d$region)
own <- clean(d$countryborn); mother <- clean(d$countrybornmo); father <- clean(d$countrybornfa)
compare <- function(birth) {
same <- birth == receiving
if (uk == "detailed") {
ambiguous <- !is.na(birth) & !is.na(receiving) &
((birth == "GB" & grepl("^GB-", receiving)) |
(receiving == "GB" & grepl("^GB-", birth)))
same[ambiguous] <- NA
}
same
}
self_same <- compare(own); mother_same <- compare(mother); father_same <- compare(father)
# Three-valued logic preserves unresolved information; no missing-to-native recode.
migration <- !self_same | !mother_same | !father_same
generation <- ifelse(!is.na(self_same) & !self_same, "1.5",
ifelse(!is.na(self_same) & self_same & (!mother_same | !father_same), "2", NA_character_))
origin <- rep(NA_character_, nrow(d))
first <- which(generation == "1.5"); second <- which(generation == "2")
origin[first] <- own[first]
maternal_foreign <- !is.na(mother_same) & !mother_same
origin[second] <- ifelse(maternal_foreign[second], mother[second], NA_character_)
# Paternal fallback only if mother is missing or known native.
fallback <- second[is.na(mother[second]) | (!is.na(mother_same[second]) & mother_same[second])]
origin[fallback] <- ifelse(!is.na(father_same[fallback]) & !father_same[fallback], father[fallback], NA_character_)
if (uk == "detailed") origin[!is.na(origin) & origin == "GB"] <- NA_character_
one_native <- mother_same | father_same
data.frame(receiving_iso = receiving, own_iso = own, mother_iso = mother, father_iso = father,
migration = migration, generation = generation, origin_iso = origin,
one_native_parent = one_native, origin_in_wave = !is.na(origin) & origin %in% receiving)
}
assess_iso_2018 <- function(uk = c("detailed", "GB")) {
uk <- match.arg(uk)
path <- file.path(root, "data", "HBSC2018OAed1.1.csv")
header <- names(read.table(path, sep = ";", header = TRUE, nrows = 0))
wanted <- c("region", "countryborn", "countrybornmo", "countrybornfa")
stopifnot(all(wanted %in% header))
# 'NA' is Namibia's ISO code: never use it as a string missing-value token.
d <- read.table(path, sep = ";", header = TRUE, quote = "\"", comment.char = "",
colClasses = ifelse(header %in% wanted, "character", "NULL"),
na.strings = c("", " ", "."), strip.white = TRUE)
z <- classify_iso_2018(d, uk = uk)
counts <- c(nrow(z), sum(z$migration %in% TRUE), sum(z$migration %in% FALSE),
sum(is.na(z$migration)), sum(z$generation %in% "1.5"), sum(z$generation %in% "2"),
sum(!is.na(z$origin_iso)), sum(z$origin_in_wave), sum(z$own_iso %in% "GB"),
sum(z$mother_iso %in% "GB"), sum(z$father_iso %in% "GB"))
labels <- c("Imported", "Known migration background", "Known native respondent and both parents",
"Migration background unresolved", "Generation 1.5", "Second generation", "Exact origin assigned",
"Assigned origin represented among receiving ISO units", "GB respondent birthplace",
"GB maternal birthplace", "GB paternal birthplace")
data.frame(step = labels, n = counts)
}
who_bmi_z <- function(bmi, age, sex, reference) {
out <- rep(NA_real_, length(bmi))
for (s in 1:2) {
ref <- reference[reference$sex == s, ]
i <- which(sex %in% s & is.finite(bmi) & bmi > 0 & is.finite(age) &
age * 12 >= min(ref$month) & age * 12 <= max(ref$month))
if (!length(i)) next
# Linear interpolation in months; preserves reported fractional-year age.
p <- lapply(ref[c("L", "M", "S")], function(v) approx(ref$month, v, age[i]*12)$y)
L <- p$L; M <- p$M; S <- p$S
z <- ((bmi[i]/M)^L - 1)/(L*S)
centile <- function(q) M * (1 + L*S*q)^(1/L)
lo <- z < -3; hi <- z > 3
z[lo] <- (-3 + (bmi[i]-centile(-3))/(centile(-2)-centile(-3)))[lo]
z[hi] <- (3 + (bmi[i]-centile(3))/(centile(3)-centile(2)))[hi]
out[i] <- z
}
out
}
run_reproduction <- function() {
# Sequential descriptive reproduction: 2014 first, then qualified 2018 extension.
ref <- read.csv(file.path(root, "data", "who2007_bmi_lms.csv"))
stopifnot(all(c("sex", "month", "L", "M", "S") %in% names(ref)),
!anyDuplicated(ref[c("sex", "month")]), all(ref$M > 0), all(ref$S > 0))
# Published WHO computation examples: tail adjustment is essential.
test <- who_bmi_z(c(30,14,19), c(11,16,9), c(1,1,1), ref)
stopifnot(max(abs(test-c(3.35,-3.80,1.47))) < .025)
# Paper cut points: -2 -> category 2; -1 -> 3; +1 -> 4; +2 -> 5.
stopifnot(identical(as.integer(cut(c(-2,-1,1,2),
c(-Inf,-2,-1,1,2,Inf),right=FALSE)), 2:5))
number <- function(x) suppressWarnings(as.numeric(gsub(",", ".", x, fixed=TRUE)))
# Numeric ISO country codes in the 2014 dictionary; Belgium is pooled.
mapping <- c(`8`="AL",`40`="AT",`51`="AM",`56`="BE",`100`="BG",`124`="CA",
`191`="HR",`203`="CZ",`208`="DK",`233`="EE",`246`="FI",`250`="FR",
`276`="DE",`300`="GR",`304`="GL",`348`="HU",`352`="IS",`372`="IE",
`376`="IL",`380`="IT",`398`="KZ",`428`="LV",`440`="LT",`442`="LU",
`470`="MT",`498`="MD",`528`="NL",`578`="NO",`616`="PL",`620`="PT",
`642`="RO",`643`="RU",`703`="SK",`705`="SI",`724`="ES",`752`="SE",
`756`="CH",`792`="TR",`804`="UA",`807`="MK",`31`="AZ",`268`="GE",`688`="RS")
standards <- flows <- list()
files <- c(`2014`="HBSC2014OAed1.1_F1.csv",`2018`="HBSC2018OAed1.1.csv")
for (wave in names(files)) {
message("Constructing descriptive reproduction: ", wave)
d <- read.table(file.path(root,"data",files[[wave]]), header=TRUE,
sep=if(wave=="2014") "," else ";", quote="\"",comment.char="",
colClasses="character",na.strings=c(""," ","."),strip.white=TRUE)
names(d) <- tolower(names(d))
sex <- number(d$sex); age <- number(d$age)
bmi <- number(d$bodyweight)/(number(d$bodyheight)/100)^2
bmi[!is.finite(bmi) | number(d$bodyweight)<=0 | number(d$bodyheight)<=0] <- NA_real_
z <- who_bmi_z(bmi,age,sex,ref)
perceived <- number(d$thinkbody); perceived[!perceived %in% 1:5] <- NA_real_
actual <- as.integer(cut(z,c(-Inf,-2,-1,1,2,Inf),right=FALSE))
fai <- perceived-actual
# Conservative predeclared descriptive screen, with unscreened N retained.
valid <- is.finite(fai) & z >= -5 & z <= 5
country <- if (wave=="2018") classify_iso_2018(d)$receiving_iso else {
cno <- number(d$countryno)
x <- unname(mapping[as.character(floor(cno/1000))])
x[cno==826001] <- "GB-ENG"; x[cno==826002] <- "GB-SCT"; x[cno==826003] <- "GB-WLS"
x
}
if (any(is.na(country))) stop("Unmapped survey countries in ",wave, ": ",paste(unique(d$countryno[is.na(country)]),collapse=","))
groups <- split(which(valid),paste(country[valid],sex[valid],sep="|"))
st <- do.call(rbind,lapply(groups,function(i) data.frame(wave=wave,country=country[i[1]],
sex=sex[i[1]],n=length(i),mean_fai=mean(fai[i]),sd_fai=sd(fai[i]))))
st$centered_person <- st$mean_fai - vapply(st$sex,function(s) mean(fai[valid & sex %in% s]), numeric(1))
st$centered_country <- st$mean_fai - vapply(st$sex,function(s) mean(st$mean_fai[st$sex==s]), numeric(1))
standards[[wave]] <- st
fas_names <- c("fasfamcar","fasbedroom","fascomputers","fasbathroom","fasdishwash","fasholidays")
fas <- lapply(d[fas_names],number)
fas_ok <- Reduce(`&`,Map(function(x,maxv) x %in% seq_len(maxv),fas,c(3,2,4,4,2,4)))
if (wave=="2014") {
birth <- lapply(d[c("m133","m134","m135")],number)
migration <- Reduce(`|`,lapply(birth,function(x) x %in% 2:6))
origin_N <- complete_N <- NA_integer_
} else {
iso <- classify_iso_2018(d)
migration <- iso$migration %in% TRUE
key <- paste(st$country,st$sex)
origin_ok <- !is.na(iso$origin_iso) & paste(iso$origin_iso,sex) %in% key
origin_N <- sum(migration & origin_ok)
complete_N <- sum(migration & origin_ok & valid & fas_ok & !is.na(iso$generation) & !is.na(iso$one_native_parent))
}
flows[[wave]] <- data.frame(wave=wave,step=c("Imported","Raw survey-country codes",
"Harmonised geographic units","FAI valid before z screen","FAI valid with -5 to +5 z screen",
"Countries contributing a valid FAI standard","Known migration background (all imported)",
"Origin-sex standard available (migration background)","Complete candidate model inputs"),
observed=c(nrow(d),length(unique(d$countryno)),length(unique(country)),sum(is.finite(fai)),sum(valid),
length(unique(st$country)),sum(migration),origin_N,complete_N),
published_2014=c(NA,NA,41,NA,166798,41,14024,8638,8124),
comparison=c("release size; not published analysis N","release geography",
"reference geography benchmark","unscreened sensitivity",
"provisional reference-population comparison","reference geography benchmark",
"different filtering stage; diagnostic only","not identical: paper uses 41-origin restriction",
"2014 unrecoverable; 2018 candidate only, not validated N"))
message(wave, ": reference FAI N=",sum(valid),"; country units=",length(unique(st$country)))
rm(d); gc(verbose=FALSE)
}
st <- do.call(rbind,standards); fl <- do.call(rbind,flows)
fl$published_2014[fl$wave!="2014"] <- NA
write.csv(fl,file.path(root,"results","sample_reconciliation.csv"),row.names=FALSE,na="")
write.csv(st,file.path(root,"results","country_fai.csv"),row.names=FALSE)
# Figure 1: paired country means; no unadjusted significance intervals.
png(file.path(root,"results","country_fai.png"),width=1800,height=1700,res=170)
par(mfrow=c(1,2),mar=c(4,6,4,1),oma=c(3,0,1,0))
for (s in 1:2) {
a <- st[st$wave=="2014" & st$sex==s,]; b <- st[st$wave=="2018" & st$sex==s,]
codes <- sort(unique(c(a$country,b$country))); y <- seq_along(codes)
xa <- a$mean_fai[match(codes,a$country)]; xb <- b$mean_fai[match(codes,b$country)]
plot(NA,xlim=range(c(st$mean_fai,0)),ylim=c(.5,length(codes)+.5),yaxt="n",
xlab="Mean FAI (positive = overestimation)",ylab="",main=c("Boys","Girls")[s])
abline(v=0,col="grey75",lty=2); axis(2,at=y,labels=codes,las=1,cex.axis=.65)
segments(xa,y,xb,y,col="grey70"); points(xa,y,pch=16,col="#246B8E"); points(xb,y,pch=17,col="#C96432")
legend("bottomright",c("2014 public file","2018 descriptive extension"),pch=c(16,17),
col=c("#246B8E","#C96432"),cex=.65,bty="n")
}
mtext("Unweighted WHO-LMS construction; z screen [-5,5]. Not verified reproduction of published figures.",side=1,outer=TRUE,cex=.75)
dev.off()
png(file.path(root,"results","reference_sample.png"),width=1500,height=900,res=150)
par(mar=c(7,5,4,1))
values <- c(166798,flows[["2014"]]$observed[5],flows[["2018"]]$observed[5])
bp <- barplot(values,names.arg=c("Paper 2014","Public 2014\nWHO-LMS screen","Public 2018\nWHO-LMS screen"),
col=c("#777777","#246B8E","#C96432"),ylim=c(0,max(values)*1.17),
ylab="Reference-population N",main="Reference sample reconciliation")
text(bp,values,format(values,big.mark=","),pos=3)
mtext("Descriptive reference samples only; these are not immigrant regression sample sizes.",side=1,line=4,cex=.8)
dev.off()
capture.output(sessionInfo(),file=file.path(root,"environment","session_info.txt"))
list(flow=fl,standards=st)
}
run_power <- function() {
if (any(grepl("^--usable-n=", args)))
stop("The standard-design N override has been removed: scientific-lead escalation is required for this complex design.")
effects <- data.frame(effect = c("Origin (H1a)", "Receiving (H1b)"),
beta = c(.17, .71), lower = c(.03, .54), upper = c(.32, .89))
effects$SE_approx <- (effects$upper - effects$lower) / (2 * qnorm(.975))
effects$statistic_approx <- abs(effects$beta / effects$SE_approx)
iso2018 <- assess_iso_2018()
iso2018_GB <- assess_iso_2018("GB")
lines <- c("# Preliminary assessment: RPHB Secondary Data Power", "",
"Updated 26 October 2026. Replication base: supplied paper_to_be_replicated.pdf (Kern et al., 2020). Preliminary power instructions: Secondary data power.pdf, pp. 1-2.", "",
"## Replication specification taken from the paper", "",
"Section 1.4 defines Hypothesis 1 as positive origin and receiving associations with a stronger receiving association; Hypothesis 2 concerns generation moderation. H1a/H1b/H1c are project labels separating components of the paper's Hypothesis 1, not separate labels used by the authors.", "",
"Section 2.1: original HBSC 2013/14 reference N=166,798 across 41 countries; migration-background N=14,024 in 23 receiving countries; participating-origin N=8,638; 514 complete-case exclusions; final N=8,124 (2,603 generation 1.5 and 5,521 second generation). These are published benchmarks, not observed 2018 counts. The project's 2017/18 comparison is a temporal replication extension, not the original study wave.", "",
"Sections 2.2.1-2.2.6: FAI=perceived minus actual status (-4 to 4); actual status uses WHO age/sex reference categories at -2, -1, +1 and +2 SD. Migration background means the respondent or at least one parent is foreign-born. Origin is own birthplace for generation 1.5; maternal birthplace for second generation, with paternal fallback when maternal birthplace is unknown or receiving country. FAS III uses six items and country/age/sex percentiles grouped into quintiles. BMI covariate is described as (BMI minus WHO age/sex median)/SD; equivalence to an LMS implementation is unresolved. Country-sex means use all adolescents, then overall sex-specific centring. Age is marked standardised in Table 4, without an exact scaling convention.", "",
"Section 2.3: Model 1 is cross-classified by origin and receiving country, with both contextual standards, age, girl, age x girl, FAS, BMI z-score, generation 1.5 and native-parent indicators; fitted using Stata IGLS. Model 2 adds the two generation x contextual-standard interactions and uses MLwiN MCMC. The existing lme4 helper is an unexecuted approximation, not an exact implementation of either estimator.", "",
"Reporting targets: Tables 1-2 country/generation counts, Table 3 descriptives, Table 4 model coefficients/intervals/variance components, and Figures 1-2 country-sex FAI standards. The sequential descriptive reproduction section below records the available checks; immigrant tables/models are not verified.", "",
"## 1. Best estimate of the focal effect as partial r", "",
"Status: not yet defensibly estimable. The focal effect (origin coefficient, receiving coefficient, or their contrast) has not been confirmed. The paper uses cross-classified multilevel models; an effective denominator df is not reported in the available Table 4. Treating 8,124 individuals as independent and substituting N-k-1 would not establish the relevant df. The PDF directs uncovered conversion cases to the scientific lead.", "",
"Available evidence: Kern et al. (2020), Table 4, Model 1; N=8,124. Approximate SE = (upper CI - lower CI)/(2*qnorm(.975)), assuming a symmetric normal 95% interval. Rounded CI endpoints introduce approximation. The absolute coefficient/SE ratios below are not established t statistics with known df and are not partial correlations.", "",
"| Candidate effect | Coefficient | 95% CI | Approx. SE | Absolute coefficient/SE |", "|---|---:|---|---:|---:|",
vapply(seq_len(nrow(effects)), function(i) sprintf("| %s | %.2f | [%.2f, %.2f] | %.5f | %.3f |", effects$effect[i], effects$beta[i], effects$lower[i], effects$upper[i], effects$SE_approx[i], effects$statistic_approx[i]), character(1)), "",
"Once the focal coefficient and defensible df are established, the PDF's linear-coefficient conversion is effectsize::t_to_r(t=abs(coef/SE), df_error=df_error). The receiving-minus-origin contrast additionally requires coefficient covariance. No arbitrary df or logistic z_to_r conversion is substituted.", "",
"## 2. Four design questions and two validity questions", "",
"1. Nested observations requiring multilevel/mixed modelling? YES. Adolescents are cross-classified by origin and receiving countries; school/class clustering also exists.",
"2. Complex sample design needing accounting? YES. Released files contain survey weights and class/cluster identifiers. Their intended analysis use and design details need confirmation.",
"3. Mediation or SEM effect? NO for the candidate Model 1 coefficients or their contrast.",
"4. Rare event or count outcome? NO. FAI is a bounded difference score.",
"5. Original covariates unavailable in replication data, and consequences? No absent raw candidate column has been identified for age, sex, FAS items or anthropometry. However, comparable WHO BMI and FAS construction remain unvalidated. Exact origin in the original 2014 release is not recoverable from national birthplace categories alone, preventing exact original-sample and origin-standard reproduction. 2018 ISO birthplace fields require a validated receiving-country crosswalk and country-specific availability checks. A high-quality exact replication is therefore not yet established.",
"6. Cases/units fully independent of the original study? UNKNOWN. Different survey waves do not prove respondent or school independence. Countries recur; available identifiers alone cannot establish cross-wave overlap.", "",
"## 3. Tier 1 and Tier 2 power", "",
"| Required estimate | Effect magnitude | Target | Status |", "|---|---|---|---|",
"| Tier 1 | 75% of original partial r | 90% power | Pending scientific-lead method and valid inputs |",
"| Tier 2 | 100% of original partial r | 80% power | Pending scientific-lead method and valid inputs |", "",
"The PDF p.2 prescribes pwr.f2.test for standard replication designs and escalation for non-standard designs. This design is non-standard. Usable 2018 immigrant N is not established; neither full-wave N nor raw input-complete N is a usable analysis N. No hypothetical sample sizes, independent-person partial r, or standard-design power percentages are reported as preliminary project results.", "",
"For a standard design only, the prescribed calculation uses alpha=.05, v=N-k-1, r_tier1=.75*r_original, r_tier2=r_original, f2=r^2/(1-r^2), and pwr::pwr.f2.test(u=1,v=v,f2=f2,sig.level=alpha). Model 1 has nine fixed-effect slopes including age x girl, excluding the intercept; that count does not resolve multilevel effective df.", "",
"## Required scientific-lead escalation", "",
"Triggered by YES to questions 1 and 2. The PDF p.1 says: 'If any are answered yes, please escalate this case to the scientific lead'. Request confirmation of the focal effect, an appropriate partial-r conversion/effective df, handling of the complex design, usable replication sample definition, and the power method for both tiers. Resolve measurement comparability and sample independence with the data provider as needed.", "",
"This request is documented locally and has not been sent. The preliminary assessment is prepared; effect-size conversion and power estimates remain unresolved rather than being filled with assumed inputs. No confirmatory replication models were run.")
lines <- c(lines, "", "## Scope: 2014 and 2018 only", "",
"The 2001/02, 2005/06 and 2009/10 public releases were previously assessed and excluded: respondent/parental birthplace fields and the bathroom/dishwasher items required for FAS III are unavailable. At the user's direction these files are no longer read or tested by the replication script. Their raw files remain unchanged; they do not contribute to replication sample sizes or power.")
lines <- c(lines, "", "## 2018 ISO-based migration and origin assessment", "",
"Primary specification: retain UK constituent-nation codes (GB-ENG, GB-SCT and GB-WLS) to match the paper's geographic units. Broad GB cannot identify a constituent-nation origin and remains unresolved where that detail is needed. Broad GB birthplace outside the UK can establish foreign birth without assigning a detailed origin. Belgium BE-VLG/BE-WAL is combined as BE in both specifications.", "",
"Sensitivity specification: combine GB and all GB-* codes as GB in receiving country and all three birthplace fields. Within-UK moves then do not count as international migration. This is a geographic deviation from the paper, not the primary definition. Country-sex FAI standards must be recomputed from pooled GB observations, and origin/receiving random effects must use GB consistently; separate-nation standards must not simply be relabelled.", "",
"| ISO classification step | Primary: detailed UK | Sensitivity: combined GB |", "|---|---:|---:|",
vapply(seq_len(nrow(iso2018)), function(i) sprintf("| %s | %d | %d |", iso2018$step[i], iso2018$n[i], iso2018_GB$n[i]), character(1)), "",
"Generation 1.5 uses own foreign birthplace. Second generation requires own native birthplace and at least one known foreign-born parent. Origin follows the paper's assignment order at the chosen geographic level: own birthplace for generation 1.5; maternal birthplace for second generation, falling back to paternal only when maternal birthplace is missing or known native. An ambiguous broad-GB maternal birthplace in the primary specification is not treated as missing or native to force paternal fallback. Missing values never imply native birth. Native-parent indicator means at least one parent is known native. The literal ISO code NA denotes Namibia and is preserved. GB birthplace counts in the primary column refer only to broad GB records; in the sensitivity column they include all pooled UK codes.", "",
"These are migration-input counts, not final eligible N: represented-origin counts do not yet require a valid country-sex FAI standard, WHO anthropometry or complete covariates. ISO-shaped codes and geographic crosswalks still require review against released documentation. This establishes a reproducible candidate 2018 migration sample; it does not resolve the 2014 national-category limitation or the power-method escalation.")
if (!exists("reproduction_results", inherits=TRUE) && file.exists(file.path(root,"results","sample_reconciliation.csv")))
reproduction_results <- list(flow=read.csv(file.path(root,"results","sample_reconciliation.csv")))
if (exists("reproduction_results", inherits=TRUE)) {
f <- reproduction_results$flow
lines <- c(lines,"","## Sequential reproduction: 2014 first, then 2018 descriptives","",
"**2014 verdict: exact reproduction is not established.** The supplied public birthplace categories cannot reproduce exact origins, so immigrant Tables 1-4 and their model coefficients are not verified. The supplied release also lacks Lithuania, which appears in the paper's Table 2; pooling its two Belgian regions yields 40 rather than 41 geographic units. The observed reference-population counts below provide a separate, reproducible descriptive check. A mismatch does not by itself show an error in the paper; release versions, exclusions and scoring conventions may differ.","",
"| Stage | 2014 observed | Published 2014 benchmark | 2018 observed |","|---|---:|---:|---:|",
vapply(which(f$wave=="2014"),function(i) {
j <- which(f$wave=="2018" & f$step==f$step[i])
display <- function(x) if(is.na(x)) "Unavailable" else format(x,trim=TRUE,scientific=FALSE)
sprintf("| %s | %s | %s | %s |",f$step[i],display(f$observed[i]),display(f$published_2014[i]),display(f$observed[j]))
},character(1)),"",
"The benchmark columns refer to different documented stages: 14,024 is the paper's selected immigrant sample, whereas the observed migration row covers all imported records; 8,638 is the paper's participating-origin restriction, whereas the observed 2018 row requires an available origin-sex FAI standard. These are diagnostic comparisons, not claims of equivalent filters. Country codes pool Belgian regions and retain UK constituent nations. The complete 2018 count is only a candidate based on observed raw covariates and standards, not a verified analysis N or power input.","",
"**Construction used for this diagnostic:** BMI from kg/cm; sex-specific WHO 2007 LMS parameters linearly interpolated at age*12 months; WHO tail adjustment beyond +/-3; paper's five actual-status categories; FAI=perceived minus actual. A provisional z screen [-5,5] is applied equally across waves, with unscreened counts retained. This screen and LMS convention have not been verified against author code. FAS checks here validate six item codes only; quintile construction and age scaling are not yet implemented. Country means are unweighted, include immigrants, and use valid FAI reference records before immigrant restrictions. Both person-weighted and country-weighted sex-specific grand centring are included in the country table; neither has been selected as the verified paper convention. The paper's median/SD wording for its BMI covariate remains unresolved.","",
"WHO source: [official BMI-for-age reference tables](https://www.who.int/tools/growth-reference-data-for-5to19-years/indicators/bmi-for-age) and [LMS/tail computation instructions](https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/child-growth/growth-reference-5-19-years/computation.pdf). The compact data/who2007_bmi_lms.csv retains Month/L/M/S from the official boys/girls expanded XLSX tables downloaded on 26 October 2026; no participant data are sent externally. Computation is checked against WHO's three worked examples.","",
"**2018 status:** descriptive extension only. Because 2014 immigrant-model reproduction failed the data-availability gate, no 2018 regression coefficients are estimated. Reproduction requires detailed 2014 origin data/national mappings and clarification of original exclusions/WHO implementation before claiming Tables 1-4 are reproducible. Model/power complexity review remains pending. Outcomes have now been examined descriptively; no prospective preregistration is claimed.","",
"[Sample reconciliation table](sample_reconciliation.csv) | [Country-sex FAI table](country_fai.csv)","",
"![Reference sample reconciliation](reference_sample.png)","",
"![Country-sex FAI means in 2014 and 2018](country_fai.png)")
}
if (file.exists(file.path(root,"results","country_fai.csv"))) {
cs <- read.csv(file.path(root,"results","country_fai.csv"))
a <- cs[cs$wave==2014,]
boys <- a[a$sex==1,]; girls <- a[a$sex==2,]
paired <- merge(boys,girls,by="country",suffixes=c("_boys","_girls"))
lowest <- boys[which.min(boys$mean_fai),]; highest <- girls[which.max(girls$mean_fai),]
gap_count <- sum(paired$mean_fai_girls>paired$mean_fai_boys)
r2 <- cor(paired$mean_fai_boys,paired$mean_fai_girls)^2
n14 <- sum(a$n)
lines <- c(lines,"","## Direct comparison with paper_to_be_replicated.pdf","",
"Checked against Section 2.1 (p.3), Sections 2.2-2.3 and Tables 1-3 (pp.4-5), Figures 1-2 (p.6), and Table 4 (p.7). Results below concern the current public release and the documented provisional scoring, not author-equivalent analytic data.","",
"### Reference population and Figures 1-2","",
"| Paper claim / statistic | Paper | Current 2014 check | Assessment |","|---|---|---|---|",
sprintf("| Reference-population N | 166,798 | %s; difference %+d (%.2f%%) | Does not match under current construction |",format(n14,big.mark=","),n14-166798,100*(n14-166798)/166798),
sprintf("| Participating geographic units | 41 | %d after pooling Belgian regions | Lithuania in paper Table 2 is absent from supplied release |",length(unique(a$country))),
sprintf("| Lowest boys' country mean (Section 3.1) | Greece, approximately -0.4 | %s, %.3f | Country and rounded magnitude can be compared descriptively |",lowest$country,lowest$mean_fai),
sprintf("| Highest girls' country mean (Section 3.1) | Poland, approximately +0.6 | %s, %.3f | Country and rounded magnitude can be compared descriptively |",highest$country,highest$mean_fai),
sprintf("| Girls' mean exceeds boys' within country | All 41 countries; paper reports significance | %d of %d paired countries have a positive mean difference | Direction only; significance has not been reproduced |",gap_count,nrow(paired)),
sprintf("| Shared variance between country means by sex | Approximately 68%% | %.1f%% (unweighted squared Pearson correlation across %d paired countries) | Descriptive comparison with different coverage; not the paper's exact result |",100*r2,nrow(paired)),
"| Girls significantly above zero | 37 of 41 countries | Not estimated | Point-estimate signs do not reproduce confidence-interval tests |",
"| Boys significantly above / below zero | 9 / 24 countries | Not estimated | No survey-adjusted intervals or matching original inference available |","",
"The current figure overlays 2014 and 2018 point means. The paper's Figures 1-2 show 2014 means with 95% intervals. Thus our figure is a diagnostic comparison, not a numerical reproduction of those figures; no exact country values were inferred by digitising the PDF.","",
"### Tables 1-3: immigrant sample","",
"| Published target | Paper value | Current verification |","|---|---|---|",
"| Complete-case immigrant sample | 8,124 | Not reconstructed: exact 2014 origins unavailable |",
"| Generations 1.5 / second | 2,603 / 5,521 | Not verified on the published eligible sample |",
"| Receiving / origin countries | 23 / 41 | Country-specific immigrant tables not reproduced |",
"| FAI mean (SD), Table 3 | 0.19 (0.89) | Not compared with full-reference means: different population |",
"| Age mean (SD), Table 3 | 13.83 (1.65) | Not verified on the published immigrant sample |",
"| FAS III quintile mean (SD), Table 3 | 3.09 (1.43) | Published quintile algorithm and eligible sample unresolved |",
"| BMI z-score mean (SD), Table 3 | -0.01 (1.22) | Eligible sample and author scoring unresolved |",
"| Girls, Table 3 | 51% | Not verified on the published immigrant sample |","",
"**Important construction discrepancy:** Table 3 reports a minimum BMI z-score of -5.56. Our provisional screen excludes WHO-LMS z below -5, so it must not be described as the paper's exclusion rule. The paper's BMI covariate is described as median/SD based, whereas our diagnostic uses WHO LMS; these may not be the same scale. The unscreened reference count is also retained in the reconciliation table. Neither the missing Lithuania records nor the screen alone can be asserted to explain the entire N discrepancy.","",
"### Table 4: regression targets (not re-estimated)","",
"| Term | Model 1 coefficient [95% CI] | Model 2 coefficient [95% posterior interval] |","|---|---|---|",
"| Standardised age | -0.03 [-0.05, 0.00] | -0.03 [-0.05, 0.00] |",
"| Girl | 0.34 [0.31, 0.38] | 0.34 [0.30, 0.37] |",
"| Girl x age | 0.11 [0.07, 0.14] | 0.11 [0.07, 0.14] |",
"| FAS | 0.01 [0.00, 0.02] | 0.01 [0.00, 0.02] |",
"| Generation 1.5 | -0.01 [-0.05, 0.03] | -0.02 [-0.04, 0.08] |",
"| One native parent | 0.06 [0.02, 0.10] | 0.07 [0.03, 0.11] |",
"| BMI z-score | -0.29 [-0.31, -0.28] | -0.29 [-0.31, -0.28] |",
"| Receiving standard | 0.71 [0.54, 0.89] | 0.80 [0.52, 1.07] |",
"| Origin standard | 0.17 [0.03, 0.32] | 0.04 [-0.19, 0.26] |",
"| Generation 1.5 x receiving | Not included | -0.19 [-0.52, 0.16] |",
"| Generation 1.5 x origin | Not included | 0.24 [-0.11, 0.60] |",
"| Receiving-country variance | 0.002 | 0.001 |",
"| Origin-country variance | 0 | 0 |",
"| Individual variance | 0.595 | 0.595 |",
"| Observations | 8,124 | 8,124 |","",
"These entries are transcribed published targets, not fitted results. Model 1 uses Stata IGLS; Model 2 uses MLwiN MCMC. The R lme4 helper does not reproduce those estimation procedures. Rounded endpoints and significance symbols in the paper should not be reverse-engineered into exact p-values. The paper interprets the receiving association as stronger from its intervals, but a receiving-minus-origin contrast in our analysis requires joint coefficient covariance, which Table 4 does not provide.","",
"**Conclusion for power:** some reference-population patterns can be checked descriptively, but neither the eligible 2014 immigrant sample nor its regression results are reproduced. The candidate 2018 complete-input count of 13,858 is not a substitute for validated usable N. Focal partial r and design-appropriate Tier 1/Tier 2 power remain pending the documented inputs and scientific-lead review.")
}
writeLines(lines, file.path(root, "results", "power_assessment.md"))
print(effects, row.names = FALSE)
message("Existing assessment updated; see results/ for available tables and figures. Complex-design power remains pending.")
}
if ("--power-only" %in% args) {
run_power()
quit(save = "no", status = 0)
}
for (folder in c("results", "environment"))
dir.create(file.path(root, folder), showWarnings = FALSE)
reproduction_results <- run_reproduction()
run_power()
# Downstream helpers: not called by the audit. Use only after the protocol is
# resolved and approved derived data have been independently validated.
construct_fai <- function(perceived, who_bmi_z) {
stopifnot(length(perceived) == length(who_bmi_z))
perceived[!perceived %in% 1:5] <- NA_real_
actual <- cut(who_bmi_z, c(-Inf, -2, -1, 1, 2, Inf), right = FALSE, labels = FALSE)
perceived - actual
}
fit_crossclassified <- function(approved_data) {
if (!requireNamespace("lme4", quietly = TRUE)) stop("Install lme4 in the approved analysis environment.")
required <- c("fai", "receiving_standard", "origin_standard", "age_z", "girl", "fas_quintile", "bmi_z", "generation15", "one_native_parent", "receiving", "origin")
stopifnot(all(required %in% names(approved_data)), all(complete.cases(approved_data[required])))
m1 <- lme4::lmer(fai ~ receiving_standard + origin_standard + age_z * girl +
fas_quintile + bmi_z + generation15 + one_native_parent +
(1 | receiving) + (1 | origin), data = approved_data, REML = FALSE)
m2 <- update(m1, . ~ . + generation15:receiving_standard + generation15:origin_standard)
b <- lme4::fixef(m1); V <- as.matrix(vcov(m1))
L <- setNames(rep(0, length(b)), names(b)); L["receiving_standard"] <- 1; L["origin_standard"] <- -1
estimate <- sum(L * b); se <- sqrt(drop(t(L) %*% V %*% L))
list(model1 = m1, model2 = m2, contrast = data.frame(estimate = estimate, se = se,
lower_normal_approx = estimate - qnorm(.975) * se, upper_normal_approx = estimate + qnorm(.975) * se),
singular = c(lme4::isSingular(m1), lme4::isSingular(m2)))
# lmer ML is an approximation to the published IGLS/MCMC analyses.
# Normal intervals are descriptive, not an approved country-level inference rule.
}
